<body><header class="topbar"><a class="brand" href="/">Access Harbor</a><nav aria-label="Setup sections"><a href="#github">GitHub</a><a href="#aws">AWS</a><a href="#login">Login</a><a href="#secrets">Secrets</a><a href="#run">Run</a></nav></header>
<main class="shell narrow" id="main"><section class="page-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">OPERATOR GUIDE</div><h1>Connect GitHub and AWS</h1><p>What to create, where to find each value, and where it belongs. Start with one private test repository or sandbox account.</p></div></section>
<section class="panel detail-panel"><h2>Choose your environment</h2><p><strong>Local:</strong> Floci supplies Secrets Manager, Cognito login, Identity Center and captured SES email. GitHub always connects to your real GitHub organization.</p><p><strong>Production:</strong> use your organization’s OIDC provider, AWS Secrets Manager, IAM Identity Center and verified email delivery. The container needs an AWS workload role; a generic Docker host does not provide one automatically.</p><p>Credentials go in Secrets Manager. Identifiers and resource mappings go in configuration files. This page does not collect or display secrets.</p></section>
<section id="github" class="panel detail-panel"><div class="eyebrow">01 · GITHUB</div><h2>Create and install a GitHub App</h2><ol class="setup-steps"><li><h3>Register the App</h3><p>Open your organization’s Settings → Developer settings → GitHub Apps → New GitHub App. Choose a unique name and use your organization’s GitHub URL as the homepage. Leave the callback URL blank, leave user authorization and device flow disabled, and uncheck Webhook Active. Access Harbor authenticates users separately through OIDC.</p></li><li><h3>Set repository permissions</h3><p>Set <strong>Administration: Read and write</strong>. Metadata read access is included by GitHub. Other permissions are unnecessary for this integration. Choose Only on this account when installing within the App’s owning organization.</p></li><li><h3>Get the App ID and private key</h3><p>After creation, copy the numeric <strong>App ID</strong> from General settings—not the Client ID. Under Private keys, choose Generate a private key and retain the downloaded PEM securely. Store its entire content, including BEGIN/END lines, in Secrets Manager as <code>GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY</code>.</p></li><li><h3>Install on selected repositories</h3><p>Choose Install App → your organization → Only select repositories → select your test repository → Install. Open the installation’s configuration page. The number after <code>/installations/</code> in its URL is your installation ID. This is different from the App ID.</p></li></ol>
<h3>Where each GitHub value goes</h3><ul><li><code>.env</code>: <code>GITHUB_APP_ID=YOUR_NUMERIC_APP_ID</code></li><li>Secrets Manager bundle: <code>GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY</code></li><li><code>config/catalog.json</code>: repository, installation ID and allowed permissions.</li><li><code>config/identities.json</code>: add <code>github_username</code> to each enrolled person.</li></ul>
<pre><code>[
  {
    "id": "github-main",
    "provider": "github",
    "name": "Engineering repository",
    "repository": "YOUR_ORG/YOUR_REPO",
    "installation_id": 12345678,
    "permissions": { "pull": "Read", "push": "Write" }
  }
]</code></pre><p>Replace the example installation ID. Add this entry to your catalog array; retain AWS entries if needed. No personal access token, GitHub client secret, webhook secret or user SSH key is required by the server. Users authenticate to GitHub with their own supported method. Invitations may need acceptance, and organization policies can restrict outside collaborators.</p>
<p>Official instructions: <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/registering-a-github-app/registering-a-github-app">Register an App</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/managing-private-keys-for-github-apps">Generate a private key</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/using-github-apps/installing-your-own-github-app">Install your App</a> · <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/rest/collaborators/collaborators">Collaborator API permissions</a></p></section>
<section id="aws" class="panel detail-panel"><div class="eyebrow">02 · AWS</div><h2>Configure IAM Identity Center</h2><p>For local development, the setup wizard provisions these resources through Floci. For real AWS, an AWS administrator must prepare an <strong>organization instance</strong> of IAM Identity Center with multi-account permissions enabled.</p><ol class="setup-steps"><li><h3>Collect instance and directory details</h3><p>Open IAM Identity Center in its home region. Record the region, instance ARN, identity store ID and AWS access portal URL. If needed, use the following read commands with your operator AWS profile:</p><pre><code>aws sso-admin list-instances --region YOUR_REGION
aws identitystore list-users --identity-store-id YOUR_STORE_ID --region YOUR_REGION</code></pre><p>Map each person’s returned UserId to <code>aws_user_id</code> in the identity configuration. This ID is different from their OIDC subject.</p></li><li><h3>Create the access level</h3><p>Under Multi-account permissions → Permission sets → Create permission set, choose policies for the access you intend to offer. Start with ReadOnlyAccess in a sandbox. Record the permission-set ARN and the target 12-digit AWS account ID.</p><pre><code>aws sso-admin list-permission-sets --instance-arn YOUR_INSTANCE_ARN --region YOUR_REGION</code></pre></li><li><h3>Give the application a workload role</h3><p>Use an ECS task role, EC2 instance role or supported platform workload identity. Grant <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on the application secret, plus <code>kms:Decrypt</code> when its key requires it. For assignments, grant the necessary <code>sso:CreateAccountAssignment</code>, <code>sso:DeleteAccountAssignment</code>, <code>sso:ListAccountAssignments</code>, <code>sso:DescribeAccountAssignmentCreationStatus</code> and <code>sso:DescribeAccountAssignmentDeletionStatus</code> actions, scoped to the intended resources as supported by AWS. SES delivery also needs <code>ses:SendEmail</code>. Both web and worker need their configured AWS permissions. Do not create static AWS access keys for the application.</p></li></ol>
<h3>Configuration</h3><pre><code># .env: metadata only
AWS_REGION=YOUR_IDENTITY_CENTER_REGION
AWS_SSO_INSTANCE_ARN=YOUR_INSTANCE_ARN
AWS_PORTAL_URL=https://YOUR_PORTAL.awsapps.com/start
SECRETS_MANAGER_SECRET_ID=access-harbor/production</code></pre>
<pre><code>[
  {
    "id": "aws-sandbox",
    "provider": "aws",
    "name": "AWS sandbox",
    "account_id": "123456789012",
    "permissions": { "readonly": "Read only" },
    "permission_sets": { "readonly": "YOUR_PERMISSION_SET_ARN" }
  }
]</code></pre><p>Add this resource to <code>config/catalog.json</code>. No AWS key or secret is placed in this catalog. Existing group/team access is preserved. Deleting an assignment does not immediately invalidate existing AWS sessions; choose an appropriate permission-set session duration.</p><p>Official instructions: <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/quick-start-default-idc.html">Identity Center setup</a> · <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/howtocreatepermissionset.html">Create a permission set</a> · <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/singlesignon/latest/userguide/permissionsetdelegation.html">Scope administrative permissions</a></p></section>
<section id="login" class="panel detail-panel"><div class="eyebrow">03 · SIGN-IN &amp; EMAIL</div><h2>Enroll administrators and requesters</h2><p>Locally, run the wizard and supply two different accounts: one administrator and one requester. Passwords are submitted to Floci Cognito, not saved in the application files.</p><p>In production, register a confidential OIDC web application with your IdP. Set the exact redirect URL to <code>https://YOUR_DOMAIN/auth/callback</code>, enable authorization code with PKCE and scopes <code>openid email profile</code>. Tokens must include <code>sub</code>, <code>email</code> and boolean <code>email_verified: true</code>. Use your provider’s supported claim inspection to obtain the exact subjects issued to this client.</p><p>Put <code>PUBLIC_URL</code>, <code>OIDC_ISSUER_URL</code>, <code>OIDC_CLIENT_ID</code> and administrator emails in <code>ADMIN_EMAILS</code> in .env. The OIDC client secret belongs in Secrets Manager. IAM Identity Center account access and the application’s OIDC login are separate integrations; do not substitute AWS SSO CLI registration credentials for a confidential web client.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "EXACT_OIDC_SUBJECT": {
    "email": "person@YOUR_DOMAIN",
    "name": "Person name",
    "github_username": "GITHUB_USERNAME",
    "aws_user_id": "IDENTITY_CENTER_USER_ID"
  }
}</code></pre><p>This is the shape of <code>config/identities.json</code>. Keep the subjects created by the local wizard. Omit unused provider fields. Add every approved person explicitly; email alone does not enroll them. A requester cannot approve their own request.</p><p>Email: local SES captures notifications in Floci. Production uses a verified SES sender with <code>MAIL_FROM</code> and <code>MAIL_TRANSPORT=ses</code>, or configured SMTP. SES sandbox restrictions must be resolved for your recipients before rollout.</p></section>
<section id="secrets" class="panel detail-panel"><div class="eyebrow">04 · SECRET STORAGE</div><h2>What to add in Floci Secrets Manager</h2><p>Run the setup wizard first. It creates the secret named <code>access-harbor/local</code> and generates the session and OIDC secrets. Open <a href="http://localhost:4500/secretsmanager">your local Floci Secrets Manager console</a>, select the secret matching <code>SECRETS_MANAGER_SECRET_ID</code>, then edit its value. Preserve existing fields and add the GitHub PEM. Console ports can differ; use the URL reported by Floci if yours is different.</p><pre><code>{
  "SESSION_SECRET": "KEEP_EXISTING_GENERATED_VALUE",
  "OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET": "KEEP_EXISTING_CLIENT_SECRET",
  "GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nYOUR_FULL_KEY\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
}</code></pre><p>The example is a schema, not a usable secret. In raw JSON, represent PEM line breaks with <code>\n</code>. Save the new version as AWSCURRENT. For authenticated SMTP, also store <code>SMTP_USER</code> and <code>SMTP_PASSWORD</code>. Production uses the same bundle in AWS Secrets Manager. Restart web and worker after secret rotation.</p><p>Never paste credentials into chat, Git, .env or catalog files. Runtime credentials are read from Secrets Manager into memory. Production setup imports an existing PEM into the manager; it does not require mounting that PEM into the running application.</p></section>
<section id="run" class="panel detail-panel"><div class="eyebrow">05 · START &amp; VERIFY</div><h2>Run the setup wizard</h2><p>From the project directory with Node.js 24+:</p><pre><code>npm ci
docker compose -f compose.floci.yaml up -d
npm run setup</code></pre><p>Choose local for Floci or production for your existing organization integrations. Existing configuration is not overwritten. Without host Node.js, run the local wizard through Docker:</p><pre><code>docker build -t access-harbor:local .
docker run --rm -it --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" \
  --network access-harbor_default \
  -e FLOCI_ENDPOINT=http://floci:4566 \
  -v "$PWD:/work" -w /work \
  access-harbor:local node /app/scripts/setup.js</code></pre><p>Then add your GitHub details above and start the local application:</p><pre><code>docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local.yaml up --build -d</code></pre><p>If you enabled the optional Floci console, include <code>-f compose.console.yaml</code> before <code>up</code> to retain its approved Docker socket mount. Only Floci receives that access. Use localhost URLs in your browser; <code>floci:4566</code> is for containers.</p><p>Production: run <code>npm run doctor -- --online</code>, then deploy web and worker with their workload role and HTTPS reverse proxy. Plain Docker alone does not configure IAM identity or TLS.</p><h3>Check the complete flow</h3><ol><li>Sign in as a requester without existing access and request 1–30 days.</li><li>Verify administrator email delivery and approve as a different person.</li><li>Check actual GitHub collaborator access or AWS assignment.</li><li>Remove access early and verify removal at the provider.</li><li>Keep a one-day request to verify automatic expiry; keep the worker running.</li></ol><p>Requests remain unavailable until configuration succeeds. Worker/provider outages can delay revocation. Monitor readiness and worker logs, and preserve the database containing grant schedules.</p></section><p><a href="/">Return to Access Harbor</a></p></main></body></html>
